## Problem

`getExtensionGalleryManifestFromServiceUrl` only guards against a null body:

```ts
const extensionGalleryManifest = await asJson<IExtensionGalleryManifest>(context);
if (!extensionGalleryManifest) {
    throw new Error('Unable to retrieve extension gallery manifest.');
}
return extensionGalleryManifest;
```

`asJson` throws on non-2xx, so error statuses are handled. But **any 200 carrying any JSON is accepted as a service index.** A body of `{"foo":"bar"}` is non-null, so it is returned, `update(manifest)` publishes it, and the status becomes `Available`. The user sees a working marketplace backed by a garbage manifest; endpoint discovery then throws later, outside this `try/catch`.

Realistic trigger: a captive portal / corporate proxy / SSO interstitial that answers `200` with an HTML-ish or JSON error payload instead of the service index.

## Fix

Validate the shape before returning. Throwing routes into the existing `catch`, which already maps to `AccessDenied` — so no new status and no new error type:

```ts
function isExtensionGalleryManifest(candidate: IExtensionGalleryManifest): boolean {
    if (typeof candidate.version !== 'string') {
        return false;
    }

    if (!Array.isArray(candidate.resources)
        || !candidate.resources.every(resource => resource && typeof resource.id === 'string' && typeof resource.type === 'string')) {
        return false;
    }

    return !!candidate.capabilities && typeof candidate.capabilities.extensionQuery === 'object' && candidate.capabilities.extensionQuery !== null;
}
```

The check covers every field `IExtensionGalleryManifest` declares as required. `capabilities.extensionQuery` matters as much as `resources`, because consumers dereference it directly (`extensions.contribution.ts`, `extensionGalleryService.ts`) — so accepting a payload without it just moves the same delayed failure somewhere else.

## Tests

Extends `extensionGalleryManifestService.test.ts` with five regressions, each asserting `AccessDenied` rather than `Available` for an eligible account served a `200`:

| Response body | Why it must be rejected |
| --- | --- |
| `{ foo: 'bar' }` | Not a service index at all (captive portal / proxy) |
| `resources: [{}]` | Resource entries missing `id` / `type` |
| no `version` | Required field |
| no `capabilities` | Required field |
| `capabilities: {}` | Missing `extensionQuery`, which consumers dereference |

Supporting changes to the existing suite: the shared `createGalleryManifest()` fixture now includes `capabilities.extensionQuery`, and the two account-switching tests that built an inline `{ version, resources }` manifest now spread that fixture and override only `resources` — without this they would fail against the stricter check.

The original two regressions (`{ foo: 'bar' }` and malformed `resources`) were confirmed to fail before the source fix and pass after, by stashing only the source change. The three later cases follow the same pattern and are covered by CI.
